.TH IPCONFIG 1
.SH NAME
ipconfig \- ask the network for an address
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ipconfig
[
.I etherN
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Ipconfig
asks for an address by DHCP and tells the stack what it was given.
With no argument, every interface the stack has that
.B /lib/ndb/local
gave no address asks for one.
With an interface named, that interface asks, whether or not it has one.
.PP
The asking is four datagrams over a conversation of
.BR /net/udp ,
bound to the interface and connected to the broadcast address,
since a machine with no address can only shout.
The answer goes three places:
.TP
.BI /net/ipifc/ N /ctl
the address and its mask, as
.BR "add ip mask" .
.TP
.B /net/iproute
the router as the default route, as
.BR "add 0.0.0.0 0.0.0.0 router" .
.TP
.B /net/ndb
one line of what was learned, as
.BR "ip= ipmask= ipgw= dns= ifc=" ,
for whoever asks the stack what it knows about itself.
.PP
On success one line is printed naming the interface and what it was given.
Each exchange waits two seconds for an answer and is tried three times.
.SH EXAMPLES
On the bench, the second card faces QEMU's router.
.IP
.EX
% ipconfig ether1
ether1: ip=10.0.2.15 ipmask=255.255.255.0 ipgw=10.0.2.2 dns=10.0.2.3 ifc=ether1
.EE
.SH SOURCE
.B cmd/ipconfig/main.odin
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR ping (1),
.IR net (2)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
The exit status is empty when the interface named was given an address,
or when nothing was asked, and
.B no answer
when a named interface asked and nothing answered.
.SH BUGS
The lease is not renewed. A router that takes the address back
after its lease will not be told.
